WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.988
This briefing comes from
Book 03 of the Federal

3
00:00:03.988 --> 00:00:07.049
Modernization Workbook,
HR-Driven Identity.

4
00:00:07.049 --> 00:00:10.812
It makes one proposal, and we'll
take it through four questions,

5
00:00:10.812 --> 00:00:14.353
the way you'd take it to leadership:
why it matters, what to do,

6
00:00:14.353 --> 00:00:18.263
who has to be involved, and how to
carry it out and know it's working.

7
00:00:18.263 --> 00:00:23.924
Book 02 made the case that identity is
now where every access decision gets made.

8
00:00:23.924 --> 00:00:26.082
This book asks the follow-up question:

9
00:00:26.082 --> 00:00:29.212
where should the facts behind
that identity come from?

10
00:00:29.212 --> 00:00:33.237
The answer is the HR system of
record, and the time to start is now.

11
00:00:33.237 --> 00:00:36.300
Let's begin with how
identity gets built today.

12
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:43.675
In many agencies, nothing
connects HR to identity.

13
00:00:43.675 --> 00:00:48.194
Accounts, access and credentials
are created and removed by ticket,

14
00:00:48.194 --> 00:00:50.050
and that costs in three ways.

15
00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.450
First, access outlives the job.

16
00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:57.197
When someone leaves, removal waits for a
ticket, yet the PIV standard says a card

17
00:00:57.197 --> 00:01:02.163
that can't be collected must be terminated
within eighteen hours of notification.

18
00:01:02.163 --> 00:01:04.050
A ticket queue struggles with that.

19
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.364
Second, records drift: the phone book,

20
00:01:06.364 --> 00:01:11.300
the manager and the approval routing
stay wrong until someone files a ticket.

21
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:15.563
And third, every joiner, mover
and leaver is help-desk work.

22
00:01:15.563 --> 00:01:18.024
Zero Trust makes the gap obvious.

23
00:01:18.024 --> 00:01:22.076
A token proves who signed it, but
not that the claim is still true:

24
00:01:22.076 --> 00:01:27.062
that the person still works here, in the
same position, for the same supervisor.

25
00:01:27.062 --> 00:01:28.699
Those are HR facts.

26
00:01:28.699 --> 00:01:31.435
And if we do nothing,
we'll rebuild the same

27
00:01:31.435 --> 00:01:34.610
manual process twice,
on two new platforms.

28
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:39.147
Why now?

29
00:01:39.147 --> 00:01:42.360
Because two migrations
open one design window.

30
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.405
On the HR side, an OMB and OPM
memo creates Federal HR 2.0,

31
00:01:47.405 --> 00:01:50.960
one government-wide core HR platform.

32
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.039
OPM awarded the platform
contract in June 2026,

33
00:01:55.039 --> 00:02:00.060
and the first agencies start
moving as fiscal 2027 begins.

34
00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:03.197
The sample timeline is about
eight months per agency,

35
00:02:03.197 --> 00:02:07.047
with the agency's interconnections
going live around month five.

36
00:02:07.047 --> 00:02:13.110
On the identity side, agencies are already
moving to Entra ID, which is Book 02.

37
00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:17.492
There, attributes drive Conditional
Access, groups and delegation,

38
00:02:17.492 --> 00:02:20.935
and feeding them from an HR
system is a supported path.

39
00:02:20.935 --> 00:02:25.448
So the window is this: design the
identity feed in during HR onboarding,

40
00:02:25.448 --> 00:02:29.572
before those interconnections go
live, not bolted on afterward.

41
00:02:29.572 --> 00:02:34.473
One honest limit: HR 2.0
covers federal employees only.

42
00:02:34.473 --> 00:02:37.504
Contractors need their
own authoritative source,

43
00:02:37.504 --> 00:02:40.890
and onboarding is the right
time to decide that too.

44
00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:45.777
So what should we do?

45
00:02:45.777 --> 00:02:51.100
There are four realistic options, weighed
against what leadership has to balance:

46
00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:54.778
the mission and the public,
security, cost and resilience.

47
00:02:54.778 --> 00:02:58.978
Compliance matters, but on its
own it isn't a reason to invest.

48
00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:02.655
Option A keeps ticket-driven
identity: no new spending,

49
00:03:02.655 --> 00:03:05.352
but the risk and the manual work stay put.

50
00:03:05.352 --> 00:03:08.777
Option B scripts automation
inside the directories.

51
00:03:08.777 --> 00:03:11.559
It's quick and cheap,
but it automates a source

52
00:03:11.559 --> 00:03:14.791
that isn't authoritative,
so bad data spreads faster.

53
00:03:14.791 --> 00:03:19.152
Option C waits for HR 2.0
and integrates afterward.

54
00:03:19.152 --> 00:03:22.241
That's defensible, but the
interconnection decisions are

55
00:03:22.241 --> 00:03:25.202
made during onboarding, so
waiting means retrofitting.

56
00:03:25.202 --> 00:03:31.590
Option D starts now, with HR as the source
of truth, designed into both migrations.

57
00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:33.802
It costs design effort up front.

58
00:03:33.802 --> 00:03:39.277
The judgment call: if HR 2.0 slipped
a long way, C becomes reasonable.

59
00:03:39.277 --> 00:03:41.814
But D's first step, data standards,

60
00:03:41.814 --> 00:03:46.020
ownership and a person key,
is no-regret either way.

61
00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.170
What does “HR as the source
of truth” actually mean?

62
00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:55.057
One owner for every fact.

63
00:03:55.057 --> 00:03:58.676
HR is authoritative for
employment status, position,

64
00:03:58.676 --> 00:04:01.883
organization, supervisor
and effective dates.

65
00:04:01.883 --> 00:04:05.649
The identity platform stays
authoritative for what it does:

66
00:04:05.649 --> 00:04:10.019
credentials, authentication, sessions,
directory objects and policy.

67
00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:13.303
And contractors, whom
HR 2.0 doesn't cover,

68
00:04:13.303 --> 00:04:17.182
get their own source,
mapped into the same model.

69
00:04:17.182 --> 00:04:21.547
That model uses OPM's standard
personnel data elements as its contract,

70
00:04:21.547 --> 00:04:25.469
so vendor field names stay at the
boundary and can be swapped out.

71
00:04:25.469 --> 00:04:29.912
It's keyed on a person key the agency
controls, and that key has to fit

72
00:04:29.912 --> 00:04:34.757
Entra ID's employeeId attribute, which
holds at most sixteen characters.

73
00:04:34.757 --> 00:04:38.659
The rule that makes it all work:
every attribute has one name,

74
00:04:38.659 --> 00:04:41.446
one definition and one accountable owner.

75
00:04:41.446 --> 00:04:46.610
When a fact is wrong, it gets fixed at the
source, never patched in the directory.

76
00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.760
Provisioning is where personnel
records turn into governed identities.

77
00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:59.047
The HR platform publishes worker
records and personnel actions.

78
00:04:59.047 --> 00:05:03.404
A small middleware, inside the
agency's own authorization boundary,

79
00:05:03.404 --> 00:05:08.770
validates each event, applies events in
order, normalizes them to the SCIM standard,

80
00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:12.348
records where they came from,
and reconciles the result.

81
00:05:12.348 --> 00:05:17.293
Entra ID's inbound provisioning then
creates and updates the accounts,

82
00:05:17.293 --> 00:05:19.723
in Entra ID and in Active Directory.

83
00:05:19.723 --> 00:05:24.423
The book sizes that interface's daily
limits against the agency's population.

84
00:05:24.423 --> 00:05:27.272
The key design choice is
that it fails closed.

85
00:05:27.272 --> 00:05:29.322
Unknown worker types are refused.

86
00:05:29.322 --> 00:05:33.536
A failed record is retried and
recorded, never silently skipped.

87
00:05:33.536 --> 00:05:35.884
And every event leaves
a provenance record,

88
00:05:35.884 --> 00:05:38.298
which is where the
evidence comes from later.

89
00:05:38.298 --> 00:05:41.284
The technology is the
example, not the point.

90
00:05:41.284 --> 00:05:45.285
Today it's Oracle Core
HCM feeding Entra ID.

91
00:05:45.285 --> 00:05:49.150
If the vendors change, the
model and the rules still hold.

92
00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:54.225
Who has to be involved?

93
00:05:54.225 --> 00:05:57.800
This is an organizational change
as much as a technical one,

94
00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:59.988
and it helps to think in three groups.

95
00:05:59.988 --> 00:06:05.292
Owners: HR owns the personnel data and
its quality, the HR 2.0 program owns

96
00:06:05.292 --> 00:06:11.226
the platform and the standards, and each
contractor source owns its own records.

97
00:06:11.226 --> 00:06:15.880
Users: the identity team runs the
directories and access from that data,

98
00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:19.509
security owns the Zero Trust
policy and the leaver path,

99
00:06:19.509 --> 00:06:23.375
and the help desk moves from
routine tickets to exceptions.

100
00:06:23.375 --> 00:06:24.387
And guards.

101
00:06:24.387 --> 00:06:26.796
The privacy office is in from the start,

102
00:06:26.796 --> 00:06:31.537
and decides the legal basis for each
leg of sharing HR records with identity,

103
00:06:31.537 --> 00:06:35.300
along with the privacy impact
assessment and minimization.

104
00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:38.746
The PIV office owns
issuance and termination,

105
00:06:38.746 --> 00:06:44.550
and the ISSO and authorizing official
own the evidence and any accepted risk.

106
00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:49.770
A data architect ties it together,
owning the standards and the person key.

107
00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:54.320
Now, how.

108
00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:59.345
Personnel actions drive the three
identity events, by code, not by ticket.

109
00:06:59.345 --> 00:07:03.307
A joiner gets an account and
baseline access, ready on day one.

110
00:07:03.307 --> 00:07:06.009
A mover loses the old assignment's access,

111
00:07:06.009 --> 00:07:09.583
gets the new baseline, and
has any exceptions reviewed.

112
00:07:09.583 --> 00:07:14.593
And a leaver is blocked first, the account
disabled and sessions revoked at the

113
00:07:14.593 --> 00:07:17.521
effective separation
time; then credentials,

114
00:07:17.521 --> 00:07:20.296
devices and access are
removed in parallel.

115
00:07:20.296 --> 00:07:23.308
The book is precise about what
the platform really does.

116
00:07:23.308 --> 00:07:25.811
Revoking sessions stops new tokens,

117
00:07:25.811 --> 00:07:29.232
but tokens already issued
last until they expire.

118
00:07:29.232 --> 00:07:32.035
Privileged leavers need
their own block path,

119
00:07:32.035 --> 00:07:36.458
because the built-in disable task
doesn't support users with admin roles.

120
00:07:36.458 --> 00:07:40.075
And synced users have to be
deleted in Active Directory too,

121
00:07:40.075 --> 00:07:42.069
or the next sync brings them back.

122
00:07:42.069 --> 00:07:47.460
Credentials follow the same events, from
PIV issuance through to termination.

123
00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:52.460
Next, access.

124
00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:56.710
Once the attributes can be trusted,
access is derived from them.

125
00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:00.898
A personnel record becomes an
attribute, a rule or access package

126
00:08:00.898 --> 00:08:05.085
turns it into an entitlement, and
an evidence record captures why.

127
00:08:05.085 --> 00:08:09.941
So every entitlement traces back to
something: a rule, an approved exception,

128
00:08:09.941 --> 00:08:13.872
an authoritative attribute, or
a privileged-access decision.

129
00:08:13.872 --> 00:08:16.122
That's what makes access explainable.

130
00:08:16.122 --> 00:08:19.131
Drift is found by
comparison, not by complaint:

131
00:08:19.131 --> 00:08:23.010
scheduled checks compare HR,
the model and the directories.

132
00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:25.653
And it's measured in
terms leadership can read,

133
00:08:25.653 --> 00:08:28.435
like the time from separation
to blocked access.

134
00:08:28.435 --> 00:08:32.816
One practical catch: Entra ID keeps
audit and provisioning logs for

135
00:08:32.816 --> 00:08:36.885
only thirty days, so the book
exports them from the pilot onward.

136
00:08:36.885 --> 00:08:39.122
And the evidence is a byproduct.

137
00:08:39.122 --> 00:08:42.452
The same records answer the
auditor's oldest question:

138
00:08:42.452 --> 00:08:46.950
does every account belong to a
current person, with a current reason?

139
00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:56.175
The execution plan has seven phases, and
each gate is the next phase's precondition.

140
00:08:56.175 --> 00:09:00.900
It runs from aligning the program,
through settling who owns each attribute,

141
00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:03.750
building and testing the
pipeline, and a pilot,

142
00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:07.800
to automating the lifecycle, cutting
over, and then sustaining it.

143
00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:09.262
Four rules run through it.

144
00:09:09.262 --> 00:09:12.412
Start now, with the HR data
the agency already has,

145
00:09:12.412 --> 00:09:16.113
and switch the source to the
new platform when it arrives.

146
00:09:16.113 --> 00:09:21.062
Run in shadow mode first, comparing what
HR says with what the directories hold.

147
00:09:21.062 --> 00:09:26.451
Cut over one population at a time, with a
signed validation report and a rollback.

148
00:09:26.451 --> 00:09:31.150
And close the ticket path only when
the data proves HR is the sole source.

149
00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:34.831
The gate thresholds, like
ninety-nine percent correlation,

150
00:09:34.831 --> 00:09:38.962
are proposals for the agency to
approve, not federal requirements.

151
00:09:38.962 --> 00:09:43.870
Chapter 13 walks an illustrative
agency through all seven phases.

152
00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:50.232
So how will we know it's
working, and what do we need?

153
00:09:50.232 --> 00:09:55.508
Five measures, each baselined in phase
one and each one something leadership can

154
00:09:55.508 --> 00:10:00.861
read: hours from separation to blocked
access, orphaned accounts trending to zero,

155
00:10:00.861 --> 00:10:05.191
fewer joiner and mover tickets,
managers and organizations in the

156
00:10:05.191 --> 00:10:09.521
directory matching HR, and every
exception with an owner and an age.

157
00:10:09.521 --> 00:10:14.384
From leadership, we need one decision:
start HR-driven identity now,

158
00:10:14.384 --> 00:10:20.133
as a design requirement of both the HR
2.0 onboarding and the Entra ID migration.

159
00:10:20.133 --> 00:10:23.885
To act on it: a sponsor across
HR, identity and privacy;

160
00:10:23.885 --> 00:10:29.298
identity named as a consumer in the HR
2.0 interconnection plan; data owners

161
00:10:29.298 --> 00:10:32.265
designated and the person key confirmed;

162
00:10:32.265 --> 00:10:35.757
and a small team for the
pre-onboarding data work.

163
00:10:35.757 --> 00:10:39.493
Book 03 has the full design,
every claim footnoted,

164
00:10:39.493 --> 00:10:43.683
and Book 02 covers the directory
migration it builds on.

165
00:10:43.683 --> 00:10:44.500
Thank you.
